================================================================================
STAGE 2 — dynamic params (add/remove at runtime), PARAMS RESYNC
================================================================================
Not built yet. Forcing case: contexts/clap_plugins.c's clap_plug_ext_
params_rescan already handles CLAP_PARAM_RESCAN_VALUES/TEXT/INFO for real
(position-only updates to existing params). Its CLAP_PARAM_RESCAN_ALL
branch (param count/order/ids changed) is still a TODO stub - the naive
fix (destroy the whole container and rebuild) is wrong: it would give
every surviving param a brand-new ContextId even when it didn't actually
change, breaking the CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT above and any UI/CX reference
pointed at a param that's still logically the same one.

Storage shape this has to work with (not one block): rt_params/ui_params
are PRM_PARAM_RT**/PRM_PARAM_UI** - each element its OWN malloc'd struct,
the outer array only ever holds pointers. "Don't shift anything" means the
outer POINTER array's slots don't move - not that some single struct block
doesn't get memmove'd (there is no such block). The natural tombstone for
a pointer slot is NULL, not a bool field inside the struct - free the
struct and null the slot, rather than keeping a dead struct allocated with
an "alive" flag nobody but this one variant would need to check.

Why NULL-and-leave, not shrink/compact the array on removal [asked and
answered explicitly, worth keeping]: compacting a middle slot means every
surviving param after it gets a NEW val_id - which breaks a real, live
mechanism, not a theoretical one: PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT messages carry a
bare val_id across the ui<->rt ring buffer, so a message written before a
resync and read after it would land on whatever different param slid into
that slot in the meantime - wrong value applied, silently, no way to
detect it. This is the exact same reasoning the CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT
above already gives for samples/plugins, not a params-specific concern -
params resync just has to follow it too. Trimming the outer array's
CAPACITY by realloc'ing away trailing NULLs (only ever from the tail,
never a middle gap) would still be safe if ever worth it - not built,
rescans are rare/cold enough that it wasn't worth the complexity.
Decoupling val_id from CLAP's OWN param_index (the fixes above/in git
history) is a SEPARATE, unrelated conclusion - it doesn't relax this one.
That was about not needing to match an EXTERNAL numbering; this is about
OUR OWN internal consumers of val_id never having its meaning change
under them. Resolving the first doesn't touch the second.

If LV2 ever needed the same resync (it doesn't today - see below): uid
would have to stop being ct_iter (position) and become something derived
from the port/control's SYMBOL instead (LV2's own stable per-port string
identifier, hashed into a uint32_t - same idea the FUTURE section below
already uses for category paths, fnv1a64(...)) - position is exactly the
thing that goes unstable across a reconfiguration, the same reason CLAP
uses clap_id instead of param_index. Not automatic, would need a
deliberate change in plugins.c's own param_add_param call. Stays
hypothetical: LV2 core (and the patch/parameters/port-props extensions
this app already uses) has no add/remove-ports mechanism at all -
plugins.c's own comment already states plug->controls "never reorders/
resyncs after load", so params_container_resync has no live LV2 caller,
only CLAP's RESCAN_ALL.

Ring-buffer race against slot reuse [ALREADY CLOSED, no pass 0 needed -
see the uid-split DONE entry above]: a PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT message written
before a resync and read after it could, if its val_id got freed and
reused for a different param in between, get silently applied to that
new, unrelated occupant. This used to require a mandatory "drain both
ring buffers before touching any slot" first pass to close - no longer
does, now that uid is this container's own minted, never-reused identity
rather than CLAP's reusable clap_id: PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT carries uid
alongside val_id, and param_msgs_process discards a message outright if
the target slot's current uid doesn't match. A reused slot ALWAYS gets a
brand new uid (next_param_uid never repeats), so this check can't be
fooled by legitimate id reuse the way it could have been when uid and
clap_id were the same thing - draining order relative to resync no longer
matters for correctness. (param_msgs_process still needs the NULL guard
noted below regardless, for a slot that's freed but not yet reused.)
Scope of this guarantee, worth stating plainly: it protects PARAM_RING_
DATA_BIT messages specifically, not "any code holding a val_id across
time" in general - the uid check only runs on that one path. Nothing
today is exposed outside it because of discipline, not a mechanism:
everything that needs to reference a param ACROSS TIME already resolves
through uid fresh each call rather than caching val_id long-term (the RT
event loop re-walks 0..param_return_num_params every audio cycle, RESCAN_
VALUES/INFO re-resolve every call - nothing stashes a val_id between
cycles). val_id stays what it was designed to be: a fast handle for a
caller that already knows where a param lives RIGHT NOW, never something
to hold onto past that call. STAGE 3's future child_at/CX work needs to
keep following this same discipline once params become CX children -
resolve val_id from uid at the point of use, don't cache the raw val_id
inside anything that outlives one call.

Algorithm (DECIDED) - params_container_resync(container, new_params[],
new_count), called by the owner (CLAP's RESCAN_ALL handler today) with its
current full param list, matched by uid not position:
  pass 1 (removals) - for each val_id whose current uid is no longer in
    new_params (skip val_ids already NULL): free(rt_params[val_id]);
    rt_params[val_id] = NULL; same for ui_params[val_id]. Nothing else at
    that index moves, and num_of_params_rt/ui do NOT shrink - the slot
    stays a valid, NULL, reusable index.
  pass 2 (additions) - for each new_params[i] with no matching alive
    (non-NULL) slot: find the first NULL slot by scanning rt_params (ui_
    params is NULL at the same index in lockstep) and malloc fresh PRM_
    PARAM_RT/PRM_PARAM_UI into it - same field population param_add_param
    already does (PRM_PARAM_RT: val, min_val, max_val, val_changed=0, uid,
    owner_id, cookie; PRM_PARAM_UI: val, min_val, max_val, def_val=val,
    inc_am, name, uid, owner_id, is_hidden=0) - uid minted from the SAME
    next_param_uid counter clap_plug_params_create seeded (see that DONE
    entry's caveat: must not reset here). No NULL slot found -> grow both
    outer arrays by one (realloc), same as param_add_param does for a
    brand-new index today.
  survivors (uid in both) - untouched by either pass. Value/name refresh
    for survivors is RESCAN_VALUES/INFO's job already, not this function's.
  Both arrays get identical treatment, same slot index. Safe to mutate
  both directly, no ring-buffer messaging needed for the structural part -
  CLAP only allows RESCAN_ALL while the plugin is deactivated (existing
  `if (plug->plug_inst_activated == 1) return;` guard), so the rt thread
  isn't reading rt_params for that plugin while this runs.
  This function does NOT report what changed to anyone - it only leaves
  the container in the correct new state. STAGE 3's CX resync discovers
  what changed later by re-walking child_at and diffing, same as it
  already does for every other container.
Needs: every accessor in params.c gets a NULL check on the val_id it's
about to dereference, once tombstoning exists - today they all assume
every index below num_of_params_rt/ui is a valid, non-NULL pointer, which
stops being true the moment pass 1 can leave a hole. This is real, not
optional: e.g. param_get_if_any_changed_rt's loop would dereference a
freed pointer on its very first NULL slot without it. Anything that walks
0..num_of_params (param_get_if_any_changed_rt, a future params-as-CX
child_at) needs to skip NULL slots the same way sampler.c's own child_at
already walks its samples array's gaps. param_find_uid already exists and
is reused as-is (its own loop needs the same NULL guard). param_msgs_
process needs the same guard on the single slot it writes into
(rt_params[cur_bit.val_id]/ui_params[cur_bit.val_id]) before its (already
implemented) uid comparison can even run - a NULL slot has no ->uid to
read; the ring buffer mechanism itself (ring_buffer.c/.h, PARAM_RING_
DATA_BIT) needs NO further changes beyond the uid field already added -
param_msgs_process already drains everything currently queued in one call
(reads exactly ring_buffer_return_items() items) - that behaviour was
never the issue, only the missing NULL guard on what it dereferences is.
Array growth: realloc to exact size on demand (rescans are rare/cold, not
a hot path) -
no doubling-growth needed.

val_id vs CLAP's own param_index across a resync [CHECKED against every
call site, not assumed] - a DIFFERENT axis from the NULL-check requirement
just above, not a contradiction of it: that one is about val_id's own
internal liveness within OUR container (a val_id can now point at a dead
slot, needs a guard); this one is about whether val_id needs to track
CLAP's SEPARATE index numbering (param_index, the second arg to get_info)
- it doesn't, and never really did past creation. val_id happening to
equal CLAP's param_index at creation time isn't a maintained invariant -
it's a free byproduct of the discovery loop walking CLAP's indices
0..count-1 while appending one param per iteration, useful only inside
that SAME loop (one counter serves as both "which CLAP index to query"
and "which val_id this becomes"). Nothing after that loop reads val_id
expecting it to still equal a CLAP index, so tombstoning doesn't reopen
anything here - it only reinforces the NULL-check requirement above, on
its own separate axis. Confirmed against every call site, not assumed:
  - Every per-param call that happens AFTER creation already goes through
    the stable clap_id (== our uid), never a live index: value_to_text
    (clap_plug_params_value_to_text, via param_get_uid), get_value
    (RESCAN_VALUES branch, via param_get_uid), and the RT event loop's
    param_val.cookie/param_val.param_id (via param_cookie_return_rt/
    param_get_uid). None of these pass val_id to CLAP as an index.
  - clap_plug_params_create's discovery loop hands val_id to CLAP as
    param_index too, but that's inherently a fresh, one-time index<->uid
    mapping built at that moment - nothing to keep in sync afterward.
  - RESCAN_INFO's get_info call [WAS A REAL BUG, FOUND AND FIXED, not
    just a theoretical risk] used to assume container val_id == CLAP's
    live param_index too, same as the discovery loop. First analysis here
    called this "safe" reasoning that RESCAN_INFO's OWN trigger never
    implies a count/order change (true per the vendored header - "some
    parameters were added or removed" is explicitly RESCAN_ALL-only) -
    but that's the wrong thing to check. What matters isn't whether THIS
    RESCAN_INFO call reorders anything, it's whether a RESCAN_ALL EVER
    happened earlier in the container's lifetime: CLAP is free to renumber
    its own param_index space on ALL, while this container deliberately
    keeps a surviving param's val_id FIXED (that's the whole point of the
    no-shift tombstone rule) - so "container val_id N == CLAP's live index
    N" is only true up until the FIRST RESCAN_ALL a plugin instance ever
    goes through, not forever. After that, every later RESCAN_INFO call
    (even one that changes nothing structural itself) could silently fetch
    one param's real info and apply it to a DIFFERENT param's slot in this
    container - or, once STAGE 2's NULL tombstones exist, dereference a
    freed slot outright.
    Fixed [DONE, doesn't need STAGE 2 built first]: RESCAN_INFO no longer
    walks this container's own val_id space and reuses it as CLAP's index.
    It walks CLAP's OWN current index space fresh (clap_params->count(),
    0..that-1) every call, and resolves each param BACK to this
    container's val_id via its stable clap_id (param_info.id == this
    container's uid) through param_find_uid - the same "always resolve
    through the stable id, never trust index alignment" pattern RESCAN_
    VALUES already used (param_get_uid, forward direction: val_id->uid).
    RESCAN_INFO needed the reverse direction (clap_index->uid->val_id)
    since get_info only accepts an index, not a clap_id - no id-based
    alternative exists for it in the CLAP params extension, unlike get_
    value/value_to_text. Verified: make, gcc -Wall -Wextra on clap_
    plugins.c (only pre-existing, unrelated warnings), valgrind against
    the built binary's actual startup path - 0 errors, 0 bytes definitely
    lost.
  - So the only actual gap: something must walk CLAP's CURRENT index
    space fresh (0..new count()) after a RESCAN_ALL to build the
    new_params[] array params_container_resync takes as input (name, min,
    max, inc, uid=param_info.id, cookie per current index) - structurally
    identical to clap_plug_params_create's own discovery loop. Worth
    factoring both into one shared "discover current CLAP params" helper
    rather than duplicating that loop, when this gets built.
  - LV2 has no equivalent problem at all, so params_container_resync
    would never be called for an LV2 container: plug->controls is
    discovered once at load and never reorders/resyncs afterward (LV2
    core has no dynamic add/remove-ports mechanism, unlike CLAP's params
    extension) - already why STAGE 2 was scoped to the CLAP RESCAN_ALL
    case specifically, not a gap in this design.

